flcrash -- the "Something went wrong" screen
============================================

Shown when the desktop session ends badly: flcomp killed by a signal (crash,
out of memory) or exiting with a non-zero status. /etc/profile.d/desktop.sh
(flcomp package) runs it after flcomp and acts on its exit status.

No compositor is left at that point, so flcrash draws on the display itself:
a KMS dumb buffer (device access through libseat, like flcomp; or the card
directly when there is no seat daemon), keys through libinput. Fonts, canvas
and the logo paths come from libflui. Without a usable display it prints the
same screen as text on the console (truecolor escapes, which the Linux console
maps to its palette) and reads the keys from the terminal.

The screen: a solid FreeLFS green field, a wide stretched "oh no.", a big ":/",
the plain headline "Something went wrong", one sentence on what happened, the
key hints, and the technical lines: Code (FL-SIGSEGV, FL-SIGKILL, FL-START for
a desktop that could not start in its first 5 seconds, FL-EXITn), Program,
Status (exit status, signal), how long it ran, the log path. Bottom right: the
FreeLFS mark with its middle bar knocked out of place. The face and the wide
line are the EMOTICON, OHNO and WIDE_X defines at the top of flcrash.c.

Keys
  Enter   exit 0: desktop.sh starts the desktop again
  R       restart the computer (flpower restart, else /usr/sbin/reboot)
  S       the end of the session log; Up, Down, Page Up, Page Down, Home, End
          scroll, Esc or S go back
  Esc     exit 3: leave to the shell prompt on tty1
  Ctrl-Alt-F1..F10 switch virtual terminal (when it has a seat)

Usage
  flcrash --status N [--prog NAME] [--secs N] [--log FILE]
  flcrash --text                 console version
  flcrash --demo [--status N] [--view log] [--size WxH]
                                 made-up data; FLCRASH_PPM=FILE writes the
                                 picture there and exits (headless previews)

The log is $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flcomp.log: desktop.sh sends flcomp's stderr there,
and with it the panel's and every program the session starts. flcrash keeps a
copy of its tail in ~/.local/state/freelfs/last-crash.log.

Build: make; make install (PREFIX, DESTDIR). Needs libflui, libseat, libinput, libdrm.

The egg: "You can't do that."
-----------------------------
`flcrash --cant PATH...` is asked before someone deletes a vital system folder: /sysflfs and its bin,
sbin, lib, libexec, share, settings, data, /boot, /, and the classic hidden links (/usr, /usr/bin,
/etc, /var, /bin, /sbin, /lib, /lib64, /home, /root, /drives, ...). The folder itself, not what is
inside it; names are folded (. .. // trailing /) and links followed (FLCRASH_PROTECT=/a:/b adds paths
for tests). The screen is the crash screen's layout and green with the stretched "oh no.", the face
`>:(` and only "You can't do that.", and two buttons: "Never mind" (default: Enter, Esc, N) and
"Continue" (C, Tab then Enter, or a click), which lets the caller really delete. Exit status 0: go
ahead (not a vital path, or Continue), 1: Never mind. On the desktop (WAYLAND_DISPLAY) it is one
full-screen window and plays the error sound (flsound); on a console the same words in text; with
neither (no terminal, no desktop) the answer is Never mind. `--text` forces the console version,
FLCRASH_PPM=FILE (with --cant PATH or --always, --focus continue, --size WxH) writes the picture.
Callers: flfiles (Delete and Move to Trash, one question for the whole selection) and an
interactive flsh before the external `rm` with -r, -R or --recursive (scripts and `flsh -c` are never
asked, coreutils' rm is not modified). tests/egg.py checks the paths, the console keys, flsh and,
with a compositor, the window (needs python3; see its header for the environment).
